import WordMeaning from '../../components/WordMeaning.astro';

const sections = [
	{
		id: 'what-righteousness-means',
		heading: 'What righteousness means',
		bodyHtml: `<p><em>Righteousness</em> means <strong>being right: right with our Father, and right in how we treat one another</strong>. In the Bible it is less a private moral score than a relationship set right. It describes who our Father is (always faithful to His word and His people), the standing He gives to those who trust Him, and the life that grows out of that standing.</p><p>Jesus puts it first among a child&rsquo;s priorities: &ldquo;But seek first our Father&rsquo;s kingdom and his righteousness, and all these will be added to you&rdquo; (Matthew 6:33). It is <em>his</em> righteousness we seek, not a righteousness we manufacture.</p>`,
	},
	{
		id: 'hebrew-and-greek',
		heading: 'The Hebrew and Greek words behind “righteousness”',
		bodyHtml: `<p>The Hebrew word is <em>tsedaqah</em>, and a righteous person is <em>tsaddiq</em>. The idea is faithfulness to a relationship: doing what a covenant asks, keeping your word, setting wrongs right. That is why righteousness and justice so often appear side by side in the prophets.</p><p>The Greek word is <em>dikaiosyn&#275;</em>, from the same family as <em>dikaio&#333;</em>, &ldquo;to justify&rdquo; or &ldquo;to declare right.&rdquo; When Paul says believers are &ldquo;justified,&rdquo; he is using the righteousness word: &ldquo;So, since we are made right by faith, we have peace with our Father through our Lord Jesus Christ&rdquo; (Romans 5:1). Father&rsquo;s Heart Bible&trade; says plainly what the word means there: <em>made right</em>.</p>`,
	},
	{
		id: 'credited-not-earned',
		heading: 'Righteousness is credited, not earned',
		bodyHtml: `<p>The pattern starts with Abraham: &ldquo;Abram believed our Father, and our Father credited it to him as righteousness&rdquo; (Genesis 15:6). Abraham did not achieve right standing; he trusted, and it was counted to him.</p><p>Isaiah is honest about the alternative: &ldquo;all our righteous deeds are like a filthy garment&rdquo; (Isaiah 64:6). So Paul announces a righteousness that comes from outside us: &ldquo;This righteousness of our Father comes through faith in Jesus Christ, to all who believe&rdquo; (Romans 3:22). He wanted to be found &ldquo;not having my own righteousness from the law, but that through faith in Christ&mdash;the righteousness from our Father based on faith&rdquo; (Philippians 3:9).</p>`,
	},
	{
		id: 'the-great-exchange',
		heading: 'The great exchange',
		bodyHtml: `<p>The most astonishing verse on righteousness is 2 Corinthians 5:21: &ldquo;For us he made him who knew no sin to be sin, so that in him we might become the righteousness of our Father.&rdquo; The Son took what was ours, and we receive what is His. Isaiah saw it coming: &ldquo;by his knowledge my righteous servant will make many righteous, and he will bear their sins&rdquo; (Isaiah 53:11).</p><p>This is why the gospel is good news. In it, Paul says, &ldquo;our Father&rsquo;s righteousness is revealed, from faith to faith, as it is written: &lsquo;The righteous will live by faith&rsquo;&rdquo; (Romans 1:17).</p>`,
	},
	{
		id: 'living-it-out',
		heading: 'Living righteously',
		bodyHtml: `<p>Righteousness that is given is also lived. Micah sums up what our Father asks: &ldquo;to do justice, to love kindness, and to walk humbly with your Father&rdquo; (Micah 6:8). And Jesus promises that the longing itself is blessed: &ldquo;Blessed are those who hunger and thirst for righteousness, for they will be satisfied&rdquo; (Matthew 5:6).</p><p>Children who have been made right with their Father start to look like Him: fair to the poor, honest in business, faithful in their word. Not to earn a place in the family, but because they already have one. &ldquo;He guides me in paths of righteousness for the sake of his name&rdquo; (Psalm 23:3).</p>`,
	},
];

